#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087478 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "60c99086-75e7-5d91-b425-4e1b6f582bcc" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Vertical Asymptotes of a Rational Function"}] :question/id #uuid "e429a65a-d65d-5b3f-a054-e3ab8a61caf3" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-5299" :question/problem "Determine the vertical asymptotes of $f(x) = \\dfrac{1}{x^2-x-6}$." :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x$.\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator, we get\n\n$$\nf(x) = (1)/((x + 2)(x - 3))\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x + 2)(x - 3) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the two solutions $x = -2$ and $x = 3$. Therefore, the equations of the vertical asymptotes are $x = -2$ and $x = 3$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087479 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b18ace11-d31c-5d5a-bd2e-32e1c28af2b9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Vertical Asymptotes When the Denominator Has Complex Roots"}] :question/id #uuid "f4879578-ac07-54f8-b00f-5ab651ae240a" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-5301" :question/problem "Find the vertical asymptotes of the function $f(x)=\\dfrac{2}{x^3+x^2+4x}$." :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x$.\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= \\frac{2}{x^{3} + x^{2} + 4x} \\\\\n&= (2)/(x(x^{2} + x + 4))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we set the denominator equal to zero. This gives\n\n$$\nx(x^2 +x+ 4) = 0\n$$\n\nWe now solve the above equation using the zero product property:\n\n- Solving $x = 0$ gives the solution $x = 0$.\n- The equation $x^2 +x+ 4 = 0$ has no real solutions because the discriminant $\\mathcal D$ is negative:\n$D|= b^{2} - 4ac; = (1)^{2} - 4(1)(4); = 1 - 16; =-15$\n\nTherefore, the only vertical asymptote is $x = 0$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087480 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "53b26ee4-53af-5660-a9e6-8e5cca8b3e1d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Vertical Asymptotes With Common Factors in the Numerator and Denominator"}] :question/id #uuid "011cf746-baa5-5395-8515-3beb98ff22a4" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-5302" :question/problem "Determine the vertical asymptotes of $f(x) = \\dfrac{x-2}{x^2-6x+8}$." :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x$.\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator and canceling any common factors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= \\frac{x - 2}{x^{2} - 6x + 8} \\\\\n&= (x - 2)/((x - 2)(x - 4)) \\\\\n&= (x - 2)/((x - 2)(x - 4)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{x - 4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x$:\n$x - 4 = 0$\n\nThis gives the solution $x = 4$. Therefore, $x = 4$ is the only vertical asymptote."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087481 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b0b57da-f9af-5fab-a337-51ee4cb28f9e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Vertical Asymptotes With Common Factors When the Denominator Has Complex Roots"}] :question/id #uuid "51ff19a3-5c58-5c26-9c29-14ffc7d284db" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-5304" :question/problem "Determine the vertical asymptotes of $f(x) = \\dfrac{x^2-x-6}{x^3 - 3 x^2 + x - 3}$." :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x$.\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator and canceling any common factors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= \\frac{x^{2} - x - 6}{x^{3} - 3x^{2} + x - 3} \\\\\n&= ((x + 2)(x - 3))/(x^{2}(x - 3) + (x - 3)) \\\\\n&= ((x + 2)(x - 3))/((x^{2} + 1)(x - 3)) \\\\\n&= ((x + 2)(x - 3))/((x^{2} + 1)(x - 3)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{x + 2}{x^{2} + 1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we set the denominator equal to zero:\n\n$$\nx^2 + 1 = 0\n$$\n\nThis equation does not have any real solutions because the discriminant $\\mathcal D$ is negative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD &= b^{2} - 4ac \\\\\n&= (0)^{2} - 4(1)(1) \\\\\n&= 0 - 4 \\\\\n&=-4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has no vertical asymptotes."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244421 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b18ace11-d31c-5d5a-bd2e-32e1c28af2b9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Vertical Asymptotes When the Denominator Has Complex Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e07bcdb7-1b3b-5227-8965-90e8e187ede5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ and $x=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244423} {:answer/id #uuid "d1c605ad-69c9-580d-8218-af950816c14b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=6$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244424} {:answer/id #uuid "2112feaf-e9d9-5a3d-ae9a-465d4aa43900" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244425} {:answer/id #uuid "0ed3e03b-2b97-5daa-af26-00ec3fa514f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-6$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244426} {:answer/id #uuid "d964613e-fcda-5f09-8c63-a405fd222a5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=2$ and $x=6$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244427}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2112feaf-e9d9-5a3d-ae9a-465d4aa43900" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244425} :answer-field/id #uuid "aff3477f-6944-59c3-b10c-0345d482afc7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244422}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "046b8096-a4b3-5639-81fd-05cfaf3fcc14" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-113527" :question/problem "Determine the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}-9}{{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+6x-12}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x:$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{2}-9}{{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+6x-12} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+3)(x-3)}{{x}^{2}(x-2)+6(x-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+3)(x-3)}{({x}^{2}+6)(x-2)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we set the denominator equal to zero. This gives\n\n$$\n({x}^{2}+6)(x-2)=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve the above equation using the zero product property:\n\n- Solving $x-2=0$ gives the solution $x=2.$\n\n- The equation ${x}^{2}+6=0$ has no real solutions.\n\nTherefore, the only vertical asymptote is $x=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244428 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b0b57da-f9af-5fab-a337-51ee4cb28f9e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Vertical Asymptotes With Common Factors When the Denominator Has Complex Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "de0199e2-7a70-5d63-b48b-b1ee00afff36" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244430} {:answer/id #uuid "e06bfa31-6271-5116-a28d-2a72235284e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-1$ and $x=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244431} {:answer/id #uuid "14493949-265d-5f9b-befd-fc93ef8f9681" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ and $x=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244432} {:answer/id #uuid "bc2b53ca-808d-5360-ad2e-da312693c654" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-1,$ $x=0$ and $x=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244433} {:answer/id #uuid "2292a74f-61f9-57fe-95db-6e5d2359cde0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The function has no vertical asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244434}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2292a74f-61f9-57fe-95db-6e5d2359cde0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The function has no vertical asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244434} :answer-field/id #uuid "7741aef8-3d0c-58eb-8091-17b7b76f794b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244429}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "95984799-323b-581e-b3cf-2bbb458bdbef" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-113545" :question/problem "Calculate the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{4}+{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator and canceling any common factors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{4}+{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}({x}^{2}+x+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{{x}^{2}}}{\\cancel{{x}^{2}}({x}^{2}+x+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+x+2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we set the denominator equal to zero:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+x+2=0\n$$\n\nThis equation does not have any real solutions because the discriminant $D$ is negative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & =1-4(1)(2) \\\\  & =-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has no vertical asymptotes."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244435 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b0b57da-f9af-5fab-a337-51ee4cb28f9e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Vertical Asymptotes With Common Factors When the Denominator Has Complex Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9483f33d-2de1-5f76-8fe8-e0fca53b7172" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ and $x=\\sqrt{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244437} {:answer/id #uuid "f4640ffd-657d-529c-8dbc-10f5e811b562" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-\\sqrt{3},x=0$ and $x=\\sqrt{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244438} {:answer/id #uuid "7b29c80e-0081-5110-bde7-2ab508f0b0a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=\\sqrt{3}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244439} {:answer/id #uuid "9d936e93-b5b8-59f1-91e9-c12a6e94cc65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ and $x=-3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244440} {:answer/id #uuid "7f554535-ede4-51cf-9470-9f28aab72341" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The function has no vertical asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244441}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7f554535-ede4-51cf-9470-9f28aab72341" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The function has no vertical asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244441} :answer-field/id #uuid "0cc2b385-a50a-5c79-bfee-a5205565d2d0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244436}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e0085fda-c140-5022-a903-4aa2d0060987" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-113554" :question/problem "Determine the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{2{x}^{2}-6}{{x}^{4}-9}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator and canceling any common factors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{2{x}^{2}-6}{{x}^{4}-9} \\\\  & =\\frac{2({x}^{2}-3)}{({x}^{2}-3)({x}^{2}+3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cancel{({x}^{2}-3)}}{\\cancel{({x}^{2}-3)}({x}^{2}+3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}+3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we set the denominator equal to zero:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+3=0\n$$\n\nThis equation does not have any real solutions. Therefore, $f(x)$ has no vertical asymptotes."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244442 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "60c99086-75e7-5d91-b425-4e1b6f582bcc" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Vertical Asymptotes of a Rational Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d1499fc5-b305-56cb-b1b7-34ddf36ad460" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244444} {:answer/id #uuid "844e61c6-7a55-51f9-9cfd-e75d247c2818" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244445}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "844e61c6-7a55-51f9-9cfd-e75d247c2818" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244445} :answer-field/id #uuid "e0e6d57d-1413-5ef2-8457-acd868828b95" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244443} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04b2cdc3-11ef-5938-a6e7-82b718242216" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244447}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "04b2cdc3-11ef-5938-a6e7-82b718242216" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244447} :answer-field/id #uuid "82f202dc-dde4-5a91-bf41-f20c621179fa" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244446}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b9201242-1fd6-516c-b0f6-ef17add9a817" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-269955" :question/problem "In **ascending order**, the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{6{x}^{2}+9x}{{x}^{2}-4}$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator, we get\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{3x(2x+3)}{(x-2)(x+2)}.\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+2)(x-2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the two solutions $x=-2$ and $x=2.$ \n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the equations of the vertical asymptotes are $x=-2$ and $x=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244448 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b18ace11-d31c-5d5a-bd2e-32e1c28af2b9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Vertical Asymptotes When the Denominator Has Complex Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ec861ded-03c9-5f56-bfe6-21787fd8f3b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244450} {:answer/id #uuid "f17f0b1f-6926-5b07-afaf-eb5245098b37" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244451}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f17f0b1f-6926-5b07-afaf-eb5245098b37" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244451} :answer-field/id #uuid "151a1256-0239-5a7c-9590-a11ed93d9436" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244449}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9ae77e5d-b0f9-5b02-9b75-f438323421b2" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-270003" :question/problem "The vertical asymptote of $f(x)=\\frac{2x-3}{{x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}+5x-20}$ is $x=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{2x-3}{{x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}+5x-20} \\\\  & =\\frac{2x-3}{{x}^{2}(x-4)+5(x-4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2x-3}{({x}^{2}+5)(x-4)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we set the denominator equal to zero. This gives\n\n$$\n({x}^{2}+5)(x-4)=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve the above equation using the zero product property:\n\n- Solving $x-4=0$ gives the solution $x=4.$\n\n- The equation ${x}^{2}+5=0$ has no real solutions.\n\nTherefore, the vertical asymptote is $x=4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244452 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "60c99086-75e7-5d91-b425-4e1b6f582bcc" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Vertical Asymptotes of a Rational Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "826f3f8f-fce5-5a53-8788-7a664039f20c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244454}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "826f3f8f-fce5-5a53-8788-7a664039f20c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244454} :answer-field/id #uuid "8f0153af-058d-5fa1-8a99-a0ad23bc889e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244453} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6cb2e42c-5eb3-5dda-ac03-b18f06a9ae40" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244456}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6cb2e42c-5eb3-5dda-ac03-b18f06a9ae40" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244456} :answer-field/id #uuid "8dadeb61-47fb-5ec9-9779-cfb08f315c90" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244455}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7c02d208-ab50-59ab-9034-5180c0ab1f28" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-278534" :question/problem "In **ascending order**, the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{2}{x-1}+\\frac{1}{x+5}$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nAdding both fractions and then factoring the numerator and denominator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{2}{x-1}+\\frac{1}{x+5} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(x+5)+(x-1)}{(x-1)(x+5)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2x+10+x-1}{(x-1)(x+5)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3x+9}{(x-1)(x+5)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)(x+5) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the two solutions $x=-5$ and $x=1.$ \n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the equations of the vertical asymptotes are $x=-5$ and $x=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244457 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "53b26ee4-53af-5660-a9e6-8e5cca8b3e1d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Vertical Asymptotes With Common Factors in the Numerator and Denominator"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b97cc287-7d92-5b2f-86e3-a1dd8cb4b12c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244459} {:answer/id #uuid "4cd1a1d2-f7c4-568b-a2a1-2c56085af28f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244460}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b97cc287-7d92-5b2f-86e3-a1dd8cb4b12c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244459} :answer-field/id #uuid "e7481fd3-ff5f-51be-999e-837b4dcbeefe" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244458} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e336a1f1-a8a2-5693-a6b1-a00bc815cd9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244462}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e336a1f1-a8a2-5693-a6b1-a00bc815cd9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244462} :answer-field/id #uuid "17876d32-327c-5ed4-89d8-85a2e4d81fb3" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244461}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0121bdef-ddb8-5058-ac4a-7e4de52c39cb" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-278545" :question/problem "In **ascending order**, the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{x+2}{{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}-4x+4}$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}} $\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator and canceling any common factors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{x+2}{{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}-4x+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{x+2}{{x}^{2}(x-1)-4(x-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{x+2}{({x}^{2}-4)(x-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{x+2}{(x-2)(x+2)(x-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{x+2}}{(x-2)\\cancel{(x+2)}(x-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(x-2)(x-1)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n(x-2)(x-1)=0\n$$\n\nThis gives the solutions $x=1$ and $x=2.$ \n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the equations of the vertical asymptotes are $x=1$ and $x=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244463 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "53b26ee4-53af-5660-a9e6-8e5cca8b3e1d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Vertical Asymptotes With Common Factors in the Numerator and Denominator"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dbff07c6-1dbf-55a6-bc8e-0d01a7132d8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244465} {:answer/id #uuid "584490c8-bb45-5595-b731-1d7d57c888b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244466}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "584490c8-bb45-5595-b731-1d7d57c888b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244466} :answer-field/id #uuid "21854994-875f-54e3-bd78-266d4e50f132" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244464}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ed5b9586-6f8d-5e4e-bcf4-a37872276132" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-278556" :question/problem "The vertical asymptote of $f(x)=\\frac{2x-1}{2{x}^{2}+x-1}$ is $x=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator and canceling any common factors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{2x-1}{2{x}^{2}+x-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2x-1}{(x+1)(2x-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{2x-1}}{(x+1)\\cancel{(2x-1)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\nx+1=0\n$$\n\nThis gives the solution $x=-1.$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the vertical asymptote is $x=-1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244467 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "60c99086-75e7-5d91-b425-4e1b6f582bcc" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Vertical Asymptotes of a Rational Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "85daaa54-20bc-504b-a7a8-13bf9ff76071" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-2,\\,x=0,\\,x=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244469} {:answer/id #uuid "454909fc-6ac2-5bdc-84a7-9a0f8d3ea986" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-4,\\,x=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244470} {:answer/id #uuid "a8cb37c2-8889-5222-8b62-5bfde23abde1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{1}{2},\\,x=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244471} {:answer/id #uuid "21afbfc4-a0cf-5fb0-a552-2a79848fe24a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=0,\\,x=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244472} {:answer/id #uuid "f1b1ecb5-89be-5a55-80c1-c0f41ee7bbf2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-2,\\,x=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244473}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f1b1ecb5-89be-5a55-80c1-c0f41ee7bbf2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-2,\\,x=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244473} :answer-field/id #uuid "1ad3aa44-66d4-5f57-82bf-205ea0962f74" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244468}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "53782803-804e-502d-bcda-59f5416c14e0" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-39791" :question/problem "Determine the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{3}{2{x}^{2}-8}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator, we get\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{3}{2(x+2)(x-2)}.\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+2)(x-2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the two solutions $x=-2$ and $x=2.$ Therefore, the equations of the vertical asymptotes are $x=-2$ and $x=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244474 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "60c99086-75e7-5d91-b425-4e1b6f582bcc" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Vertical Asymptotes of a Rational Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c671d6ff-b201-592f-984f-c8b6350a7cc2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-1,\\,x=1,\\,x=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244476} {:answer/id #uuid "7f6505de-f1a8-52d6-a334-7bdc05c3815f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-3,\\,x=1,\\,x=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244477} {:answer/id #uuid "d8612c72-3c4f-5932-957f-68ddfb91d377" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-3,\\,x=-1,\\,x=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244478} {:answer/id #uuid "486c9506-60ba-525a-a798-d496693df2c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-1,\\,x=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244479} {:answer/id #uuid "590c92b3-0e25-5ee5-98b1-112d0c45cdba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-1,\\,x=-\\frac{1}{3},\\,x=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244480}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d8612c72-3c4f-5932-957f-68ddfb91d377" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-3,\\,x=-1,\\,x=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244478} :answer-field/id #uuid "ed6b8764-edab-5b6d-94cd-954f90ed2b1b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244475}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ca4d349e-c1ee-5a19-a1e0-b0c9e7e02dd8" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-39793" :question/problem "Determine the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{x}{(x+3)(x+1)(x-1)}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+3)(x+1)(x-1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the three solutions $x=-3,$ $x=-1$ and $x=1.$ Therefore, the equations of the vertical asymptotes are  $x=-3,\\,$ $x=-1$ and $x=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088105 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b18ace11-d31c-5d5a-bd2e-32e1c28af2b9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Vertical Asymptotes When the Denominator Has Complex Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ce48952f-e83e-5de6-8d03-4fd3925b941c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = 1, x =-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085562} {:answer/id #uuid "d8a20e35-4bc6-501a-a719-20e146cc68df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x = \\frac{1}{2}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085563} {:answer/id #uuid "06afdeec-de6d-5d71-b343-aee6d4096274" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x =-1, x =-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085564} {:answer/id #uuid "1cf0f315-4ac5-5e0c-859f-f25663cc6b00" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x = 0$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085565} {:answer/id #uuid "3ddb3f0d-6a42-5052-a53a-00cc70b090a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = 1, x = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085566}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1cf0f315-4ac5-5e0c-859f-f25663cc6b00" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x = 0$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085565} :answer-field/id #uuid "b4497d9e-0846-5972-aaef-078846261816" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085561}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bac7c54b-040c-5d96-8fcc-40e2d221cff2" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-39795" :question/problem "Calculate the vertical asymptotes of $f(x) = \\frac{5}{x^{3} + x^{2} + x}$." :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{5}{{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}+x} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{x({x}^{2}+x+1)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we set the denominator equal to zero. This gives\n\n$$\nx({x}^{2}+x+1)=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve the above equation using the zero product property:\n\n- Solving $2x=0$ gives the solution $x=0.$\n\n- The equation ${x}^{2}+x+1=0$ has no real solutions because the discriminant $D$ is negative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & ={1}^{2}-4(1)(1) \\\\  & =1-4 \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the only vertical asymptote is $x=0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244481 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "53b26ee4-53af-5660-a9e6-8e5cca8b3e1d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Vertical Asymptotes With Common Factors in the Numerator and Denominator"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "55a79939-ec06-5bb7-a97d-f6829ed4d8bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=0,\\,x=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244483} {:answer/id #uuid "f5ae5a22-7a44-5333-9d77-fb5d218f2c23" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-5,\\,x=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244484} {:answer/id #uuid "2c143f3f-f895-5523-bd8e-a8c5ba762b97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-5$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244485} {:answer/id #uuid "5c1eacb0-27c9-5c05-9972-65b812b5f62b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244486} {:answer/id #uuid "b538ced9-1921-5056-8a6c-f23516d3216c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-5,\\,x=-1,\\,x=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244487}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2c143f3f-f895-5523-bd8e-a8c5ba762b97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-5$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244485} :answer-field/id #uuid "8db5b8f4-851a-5371-99d7-476bf80ae9c1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244482}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "32be6274-dfa8-59f2-aaff-f909f160514c" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-39804" :question/problem "Find the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+3x-1}{(x-1)(x+5{)}^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator and canceling any common factors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+3x-1}{(x-1)(x+5{)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-1{)}^{3}}{(x-1)(x+5{)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-1)(x-1{)}^{2}}{(x-1)(x+5{)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{(x-1)}(x-1{)}^{2}}{\\cancel{(x-1)}(x+5{)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-1{)}^{2}}{(x+5{)}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n(x+5{)}^{2}=0\n$$\n\nThis gives the solution $x=-5.$ Therefore, $x=-5$ is the only vertical asymptote."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244488 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "53b26ee4-53af-5660-a9e6-8e5cca8b3e1d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Vertical Asymptotes With Common Factors in the Numerator and Denominator"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "86e6a4e9-3536-597d-ad53-dd682e7a8693" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244490} {:answer/id #uuid "033f2964-5658-5c3d-8d41-5f8b8f03f6b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244491} {:answer/id #uuid "6ffcef3c-dfff-5cc5-865f-6a93368f8aca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244492} {:answer/id #uuid "aaf56efe-3c49-56fb-a4f6-13996b0e5994" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-1,\\,x=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244493} {:answer/id #uuid "1b595521-b5ed-5849-95a6-bb85abcb2af5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The function has no vertical asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244494}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "86e6a4e9-3536-597d-ad53-dd682e7a8693" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244490} :answer-field/id #uuid "6068a0f2-0fc5-5933-91e4-4558939123aa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244489}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7f79f42d-8b5c-5d47-bdfd-d09cbfb2f9d8" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-39931" :question/problem "Determine the vertical asymptotes of the function $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}-4}{{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator and canceling any common factors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{2}-4}{{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-2)(x+2)}{{x}^{2}(x+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-2)\\cancel{(x+2)}}{{x}^{2}\\cancel{(x+2)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x-2}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we set the denominator equal to zero:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}=0\n$$\n\nThis gives the solution $x=0.$ Therefore, $x=0$ is the only vertical asymptote."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244495 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b0b57da-f9af-5fab-a337-51ee4cb28f9e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Vertical Asymptotes With Common Factors When the Denominator Has Complex Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "48e6d0f6-bb38-5af3-bc2e-8fd38f543d65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244497} {:answer/id #uuid "0dd8c48a-48dd-543e-a81f-91aa6a23409a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244498} {:answer/id #uuid "714e9903-5139-5497-a5a2-e133e2c57e34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-7$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244499} {:answer/id #uuid "20bbc078-c8db-55ef-9abd-bb183c53b3a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The function has no vertical asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244500} {:answer/id #uuid "57262577-5ada-5fee-b3d6-4e7457b374df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-2,\\,x=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244501}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "20bbc078-c8db-55ef-9abd-bb183c53b3a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The function has no vertical asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244500} :answer-field/id #uuid "1ff296ab-3b26-5c48-8f90-3081b1849439" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244496}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9d19bd23-7337-51bb-8b7d-7464ffe77faa" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-39934" :question/problem "Determine the vertical asymptotes of the function $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}+3x}{{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}+8x}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator and canceling any common factors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{2}+3x}{{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}+8x} \\\\  & =\\frac{x(x+3)}{x({x}^{2}+2x+8)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{x}(x+3)}{\\cancel{x}({x}^{2}+2x+8)} \\\\  & =\\frac{x+3}{{x}^{2}+2x+8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we set the denominator equal to zero:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2x+8=0\n$$\n\nThis equation does not have any real solutions because the discriminant $D$ is negative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & ={2}^{2}-4(1)(8) \\\\  & =-28\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has no vertical asymptotes."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244502 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b0b57da-f9af-5fab-a337-51ee4cb28f9e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Vertical Asymptotes With Common Factors When the Denominator Has Complex Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e192115-e897-5b7b-b1a5-fca98400565e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\pm \\frac{1}{2},\\,x=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244504} {:answer/id #uuid "01a4901a-d086-5832-946f-ab7856a1fa96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-1,\\,x=0,\\,x=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244505} {:answer/id #uuid "0d883f3b-2ee6-533d-8554-e299230631e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244506} {:answer/id #uuid "39edf0a9-3626-50b6-b8ac-c6377332b536" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\pm 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244507} {:answer/id #uuid "2350bf6f-029a-56e7-8eed-ce2d4597c2b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The function has no vertical asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244508}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2350bf6f-029a-56e7-8eed-ce2d4597c2b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The function has no vertical asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244508} :answer-field/id #uuid "18f59d0c-7f4f-5f14-a8bf-a98307102924" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244503}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "10e486fb-a153-567a-b122-83eab72817c1" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-39936" :question/problem "Determine the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}-1}{{x}^{4}-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator and canceling any common factors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{2}-1}{{x}^{4}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}-1}{({x}^{2}-1)({x}^{2}+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-1)(x+1)}{(x-1)(x+1)({x}^{2}+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{(x-1)(x+1)}}{\\cancel{(x-1)(x+1)}({x}^{2}+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we set the denominator equal to zero:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+1=0\n$$\n\nThis equation does not have any real solutions. Therefore, $f(x)$ has no vertical asymptotes."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244509 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "60c99086-75e7-5d91-b425-4e1b6f582bcc" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Vertical Asymptotes of a Rational Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16ce1a08-4a57-5f73-91bb-f20f8d91e4cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244511} {:answer/id #uuid "b7a175bc-8486-5624-9c0a-56fd144f31f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=4$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244512} {:answer/id #uuid "8216ba9a-7ffd-521e-a6c3-f204b4d6123f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The function has no vertical asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244513} {:answer/id #uuid "6b8b8e31-ef62-59a2-a256-857fdfe6de1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-1,\\,x=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244514} {:answer/id #uuid "f2e33ed1-896d-5533-b219-4bd9aa16612c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-3,\\,x=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244515}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6b8b8e31-ef62-59a2-a256-857fdfe6de1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-1,\\,x=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244514} :answer-field/id #uuid "6e533f30-f259-58a4-906a-25775d9771ef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244510}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b6966b01-ab60-53fc-b128-2d7e18ed9248" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-80669" :question/problem "Determine the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}+3x}{{x}^{2}-3x-4}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator, we get\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{x(x+3)}{(x+1)(x-4)}.\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)(x-4) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the two solutions $x=-1$ and $x=4.$ Therefore, the equations of the vertical asymptotes are $x=-1$ and $x=4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244516 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b18ace11-d31c-5d5a-bd2e-32e1c28af2b9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Vertical Asymptotes When the Denominator Has Complex Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bfd4a485-af18-53f2-89de-db046518bd02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-2,\\,x=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244518} {:answer/id #uuid "c1a88c2c-d0b4-5719-a479-95cee5741f53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244519} {:answer/id #uuid "559a96b6-a96c-5b10-9ddf-38fcbe5f9065" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244520} {:answer/id #uuid "ee6d8f23-7ff8-5cd6-99e8-5b8c8de6e3df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=0,\\,x=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244521} {:answer/id #uuid "3eda7e06-233e-5ff0-a938-24550d939573" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-2,\\,x=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244522}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c1a88c2c-d0b4-5719-a479-95cee5741f53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244519} :answer-field/id #uuid "b0d747a6-c6d2-5c6a-8e21-9b79e1af9248" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244517}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3a048473-3fcb-5d1a-a119-0fd9e80272ff" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-80676" :question/problem "Calculate the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{2x-1}{{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+4x-8}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{2x-1}{{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+4x-8} \\\\  & =\\frac{2x-1}{{x}^{2}(x-2)+4(x-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2x-1}{({x}^{2}+4)(x-2)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we set the denominator equal to zero. This gives\n\n$$\n({x}^{2}+4)(x-2)=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve the above equation using the zero product property:\n\n- Solving $x-2=0$ gives the solution $x=2.$\n\n- The equation ${x}^{2}+4=0$ has no real solutions.\n\nTherefore, the only vertical asymptote is $x=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088328 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b18ace11-d31c-5d5a-bd2e-32e1c28af2b9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Vertical Asymptotes When the Denominator Has Complex Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8da0888c-8864-5c72-93dd-af177420cfe9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The function has no vertical asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086899} {:answer/id #uuid "975dfde5-f5c3-5762-89a1-2b9b4cb1670b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x = 5$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086900} {:answer/id #uuid "08d259f8-683a-5aa4-8828-1cdeebf29e1b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x =-1, x = 5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086901} {:answer/id #uuid "47991dd1-4a85-52fe-864f-246f066e63e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = 1, x = 5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086902} {:answer/id #uuid "60e0580e-a88d-55d6-9c2c-7bd58d0ca5b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x = 1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086903}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8da0888c-8864-5c72-93dd-af177420cfe9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The function has no vertical asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086899} :answer-field/id #uuid "502adbc5-d4de-5126-a953-7b1272d76fb1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086898}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "90dc5317-bc84-5136-bb1e-99a7e0621f24" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-80679" :question/problem "Calculate the vertical asymptotes of $f(x) = \\frac{1}{x^{2} - 4x + 5}$." :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we set the denominator equal to zero. This gives\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-4x+5=0.\n$$\n\nNote that the above equation has no real solutions because the discriminant $D$ is negative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & =(-4{)}^{2}-4(1)(5) \\\\  & =16-20 \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=f(x)$ has no vertical asymptotes."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244523 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "53b26ee4-53af-5660-a9e6-8e5cca8b3e1d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Vertical Asymptotes With Common Factors in the Numerator and Denominator"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "73ef5ac6-2d11-5c0f-9771-005cd171f65b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244525} {:answer/id #uuid "1c72a0f1-a3ad-5fa3-b450-f3b1983bf276" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244526} {:answer/id #uuid "07ffc3fb-8526-58f8-8e45-ee45883509f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=0,\\,x=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244527} {:answer/id #uuid "dbde9959-92de-5a71-9916-adb91be4172e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244528} {:answer/id #uuid "9bccf02f-24d5-5650-ac8f-66cba72237f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The function has no vertical asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244529}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1c72a0f1-a3ad-5fa3-b450-f3b1983bf276" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244526} :answer-field/id #uuid "0446ce53-7377-5e26-b7d5-3668e465def9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244524}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4e962760-f3da-5333-9a7a-01dcad931ed8" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-80691" :question/problem "Calculate the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{x+2}{{x}^{2}+2x}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the denominator and canceling any common factors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{x+2}{{x}^{2}+2x} \\\\  & =\\frac{x+2}{x(x+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{x+2}}{x\\cancel{(x+2)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\nx=0\n$$\n\nThis gives the solution $x=0.$ Therefore, $x=0$ is the only vertical asymptote."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244530 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b0b57da-f9af-5fab-a337-51ee4cb28f9e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Vertical Asymptotes With Common Factors When the Denominator Has Complex Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ebbbcb59-c692-531d-9274-f1ef43b872e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244532} {:answer/id #uuid "efbbe1ba-3638-5ff3-977d-2846d0e09a5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244533} {:answer/id #uuid "8059f82e-e48a-532f-adf4-5db8e1c8ba05" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=0,\\,x=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244534} {:answer/id #uuid "05719e5e-ac47-5204-b085-16cea62b292e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-1,\\,x=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244535} {:answer/id #uuid "927be640-365c-5a50-9301-286227364789" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The function has no vertical asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244536}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "927be640-365c-5a50-9301-286227364789" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The function has no vertical asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244536} :answer-field/id #uuid "b0ad7829-a0b3-5d68-8a74-2f37bd61da38" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244531}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "efadf630-7319-56a5-b0db-31af86bef5b0" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-80696" :question/problem "Determine the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{4x}{x({x}^{2}+1)}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator and canceling any common factors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{4x}{x({x}^{2}+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\cancel{x}}{\\cancel{x}({x}^{2}+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we set the denominator equal to zero:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+1=0\n$$\n\nThis equation does not have any real solutions. Therefore, $f(x)$ has no vertical asymptotes."}]}
